Response:
["{
  "circle entries report more comprehensive advanced academic, elective, and extracurricular program offerings and demonstrable college‑prep outcomes than square entries": "These reviews describe concrete, school‑provided programs and outcomes: multiple AP/honors courses, dual‑enrollment/dual‑diploma tracks, specialized pathways (arts, engineering, JROTC/AFROTC, FFA), award‑winning clubs, and explicit alumni college matriculation (e.g., '5 AP courses...forensic science...over 80% of black male students going to college', 'enough AP courses to enter college as a sophomore', 'students went on to Princeton/Harvard/Cal Berkeley'). This bucket captures content about what the school formally offers and measurable post‑secondary results, distinct from how well individuals teach or how administrators behave.",
  "circle entries report more frequent student misconduct, bullying, substance use, vandalism, theft, and inadequate disciplinary response than square entries": "These reviews recount on‑campus safety and behavior incidents and weak enforcement: repeated bullying, gang activity and open drug/vaping dealing, athlete‑caused vandalism, routine thefts, students threatening others at public events, and administration that 'does nothing' or minimally intervenes (examples: 'trashed the theater room', 'drug/vaping problem...district trying to hide it', 'students are regularly the victim of thieves', 'there is so much bullying...staff and teachers do NOTHING'). This bucket groups claims about student safety/behavior and school discipline response.",
  "circle entries report more administrative dysfunction, favoritism, governance controversies, record/accounting abuses, and opaque decision‑making than square entries": "These reviews allege leadership and governance problems: nepotism and insider scholarship allocation, board‑level scandals and calls for investigations, mismanaged budgets and contested spending, withholding records/tuition refunds, redirecting or 'purging' failing students, and systemic non‑responsiveness to parent communications (examples: 'Scholarships go to well connected rich insiders', 'Board of Ed special meeting...impartial investigation', 'they would only refund a fraction of his tuition...would have withheld his records'). This bucket is about governance, policy, and high‑level administrative practice rather than classroom instruction.",
  "square entries report more consistent teacher engagement, effective counseling/parent communication, and proactive college‑readiness supports than circle entries": "Square reviews more often describe steady, caring instruction and supportive student services: teachers who are accessible and help students, functioning guidance/college counseling, good parent‑teacher communication and administrative responsiveness, and concrete test/IB/AP preparation that led to scholarships and college placements (examples: 'teachers and staff are very friendly', 'The counselors have been awesome in directing my daughter in applying for college and scholarships', 'teachers welcomed questions and were very available', 'IB/AP programs made college a breeze'). This bucket groups teacher/counselor responsiveness and family communication as strengths reported more frequently in square entries."
}"]

["{
  "circle entries report stronger, structured college‑preparation programs and measurable postsecondary outcomes than square entries": "These circle reviews name concrete academic pathways (AP/IB/dual‑enrollment, early‑college partnerships, college‑credit courses and magnet/AVID-style programs) and cite measurable outcomes (SAT/ACT performance, IB scores, percent accepted to college, early college credit completions). Example evidence: reviewers cite AP and college‑credit classes, an IB average score, students accepted to selective universities, and early‑college program completions; square reviews rarely provide comparable program detail or outcome statistics and focus more on isolated course fragments or complaints about rigor.",
  "square entries describe more consistent teacher commitment, classroom instruction quality, and hands‑on student support than circle entries": "Square reviews repeatedly depict classrooms with engaged, helpful, and student‑focused teachers and routine instructional supports (teachers who give clear explanations, after‑school help, and proactive guidance), while circle reviews more often record inconsistent or poor instructional practice (teachers who 'don't teach', grade inconsistently, or are unprofessional). Example evidence: square: 'teachers and staff are committed…', 'faculty is on top of it!'; circle: 'many teachers are incompetent', 'teachers won't explain material' and 'no graded tests returned.'",
  "circle entries report more severe safety and discipline breakdowns (bullying, physical fights, sexual harassment/assault, substance use) and ineffective administrative response than square entries": "Circle reviews contain repeated accounts of pervasive bullying, social‑media documented fights, frequent drug use, even sexual‑assault claims, accompanied by administrators who fail to enforce discipline, downgrade handbook penalties, or ignore parental reports; square reviews rarely describe this level of violence and often say staff actively intervene. Example evidence: 'bullying is way worse… many sexual assaults… they do nothing', 'fighting is off the charts', 'drugs are used very often,' versus square’s absence of comparable incidents.",
  "circle entries report more conspicuous facility, capacity, and operational problems (overcrowding, aging/upkeep failures, scheduling/records/billing issues) than square entries": "Circle reviewers frequently cite tangible resource and operations failures—extreme overcrowding and trailers, 40+ student classes, lack of air conditioning, outdated textbooks, construction or maintenance problems, dysfunctional online/cyber platforms, billing mistakes, transcript holds and administrative data manipulation—whereas square reviews rarely document these kinds of chronic facility or front‑office breakdowns. Example evidence: 'roughly 3,000 kids…no A/C…textbooks not up to date', 'transcript held for unpaid tuition', and 'cyber program totally useless.'"
}"]
